{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788470429-FD3eYN6Nv8iDv14XIwxh","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a full picture. The core change the user wants is in `PlanningStep.to_messages` in `/testbed/src/smolagents/memory.py`. This is the single point through which the plan is converted into conversation messages — so adding a USER message right after the ASSISTANT plan message there means:\n\n1. It appears exactly once after each plan (each `PlanningStep.to_messages()` call produces one plan message + one follow-up message).\n2. It only appears when `summary_mode=False` (the `if summary_mode: return []` guard handles this).\n3. It's automatic — every time a plan is written into the conversation, the follow-up message comes with it.\n\nLet me make the change:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass PlanningStep(MemoryStep):\n    model_input_messages: List[Message]\n    model_output_message: ChatMessage\n    plan: str\n\n    def to_messages(self, summary_mode: bool, **kwargs) -> List[Message]:\n        if summary_mode:\n            return []\n        return [\n            Message(role=MessageRole.ASSISTANT, content=[{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": self.plan.strip()}]),\n            Message(role=MessageRole.USER, content=[{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Now go on and execute this plan.\"}]),\n        ]\n```\n\nThen update the test in test_memory.py (`test_planning_step_to_messages`) to reflect the new behavior — currently it asserts `len(messages) == 1`. I should update it to assert 2 messages with the second being a USER message with the instruction text. And add a summary_mode test to verify the message isn't shown.\n\nWait — should I also update gradio_ui.py? The user says \"when my AI assistant presents a plan to me... after the assistant displays the plan content, I want the system to immediately show a user-style message\". The gradio UI is a display layer for the user. But the primary semantic is the conversation display. The user said \"This creates a natural transition that signals the assistant to stop planning and start implementing\" — that's about the LLM conversation messages (used as input to the LLM). The `to_messages` method is used both for the LLM input (write_memory_to_messages) and conceptually for display.\n\nHmm, in gradio_ui.py, `pull_messages_from_step` renders the PlanningStep directly from `step_log.plan`, not via `to_messages`. So the gradio UI would not show the user message. Should I add it there too?\n\nThe user's requirement: \"after the assistant displays the plan content, I want the system to immediately show a user-style message saying 'Now go on and execute this plan.'\" and \"only when the full conversation is being displayed (not in summary mode)\".\n\nThe phrase \"not in summary mode\" strongly maps to `to_messages(summary_mode=...)` — summary_mode is a parameter of the memory-to-messages conversion. The gradio UI doesn't have a summary mode concept. So the change belongs in `PlanningStep.to_messages`. That's the canonical \"full conversation display\" path.\n\nShould I add it to the gradio UI too? The user says \"the message should appear exactly once after each plan is shown, but only when the full conversation is being displayed (not in summary mode)\". The gradio UI streams steps as they happen — the plan is shown once per planning step there. Adding a user-role message in the gradio chat could be a reasonable interpretation of \"the system immediately shows a user-style message.\"\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most consistent with \"consistent and automatic every time a plan is generated and presented\". The core mechanism is `PlanningStep.to_messages` — this is what feeds the LLM conversation. The gradio UI is a separate rendering path.\n\nActually, let me consider: the phrase \"user-style message\" — in smolagents, `MessageRole.USER` is the role. The instruction \"signals the assistant to stop planning and start implementing\" clearly refers to the LLM receiving this message as input. The gradio display is for the human. The user says \"I want the system to immediately show a user-style message\" — this could be either, but the functional purpose (\"signals the assistant\") is about the model input.\n\nI'll make the core change in `memory.py`, and also add it to the gradio UI so the visual display matches — actually wait, let me be careful. Adding to gradio_ui might be scope creep. Let me re-read the request:\n\n\"I want to ensure that when my AI assistant presents a plan to me, it automatically follows up with a clear instruction to execute that plan. ... Specifically, after the assistant displays the plan content, I want the system to immediately show a user-style message saying 'Now go on and execute this plan.' This creates a natural transition that signals the assistant to stop planning and start implementing the steps. The message should appear exactly once after each plan is shown, but only when the full conversation is being displayed (not in summary mode). I need this behavior to be consistent and automatic every time a plan is generated and presented to me.\"\n\n\"when the full conversation is being displayed (not in summary mode)\" — summary_mode is the key discriminator here, which exists only in `to_messages`. So the change is in `PlanningStep.to_messages`. That's where \"the full conversation is being displayed\" — i.e., converting memory to messages for display/LLM input.\n\nI think the cleanest interpretation: modify `PlanningStep.to_messages` to append the USER message when not in summary mode. That satisfies:\n- \"automatically follows up\" ✓ (built into the step's message conversion)\n- \"immediately show\" ✓ (immediately after the plan message in the list)\n- \"user-style message\" ✓ (MessageRole.USER)\n- \"exactly once after each plan\" ✓ (one message per PlanningStep)\n- \"only when full conversation, not summary mode\" ✓ (summary_mode returns [])\n\nNow, should I touch gradio_ui.py? The user mentions \"displays the plan content\" — in the gradio UI, the plan is displayed. If the user is looking at the gradio chat UI, they'd want to see the message there too. But the gradio UI has no summary mode. Hmm.\n\nI think the safest, most focused change is memory.py's `PlanningStep.to_messages` — that's the canonical conversation display path with the summary_mode concept the user explicitly references. The gradio UI is a separate streaming visualization; adding a user-role chat bubble there would be inconsistent with how the UI works (all bubbles there are role=\"assistant\" or role=\"user\" for the human's input).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. \"the system to immediately show a user-style message\" — for the transition to work for the LLM, the message must be in the conversation as a user message. That's memory.py. For the human watching the gradio UI, they'd also see it if added there. But the user's stated discriminator is summary mode, which is memory.py territory. I'll do memory.py only, and mention the gradio UI display path in my summary so the user knows the streaming UI renders the plan separately.\n\nHmm, but wait — \"when my AI assistant presents a plan to me\" — the user is the human watching. And \"I want the system to immediately show a user-style message\" — \"show\" suggests display. But then \"not in summary mode\" is the giveaway: summary mode is a memory-to-messages concept. In the gradio UI there's no summary mode at all, so the qualifier \"not in summary mode\" would be meaningless there.\n\nDecision: change `PlanningStep.to_messages` in memory.py. Update the existing test and add coverage for both modes. Let me also check if any other code depends on the number of messages produced by a PlanningStep (e.g., tests in test_agents.py that count messages).","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,